   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the family court act and the civil rights law, in relation to\nname changes of children",
    "basePrintNo": "S9424",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9424-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§115 & 439, Fam Ct Act; amd §§60 & 62, Civ Rts L",
    "lawSection": "Family Court Act",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9424\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the family court act and the civil rights law, in\nrelation to name changes of children\n\n2.  \nSOURCE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill is being introduced at the request of the Judiciary, on the\nrecommendation of the Chief Administrative Judge's Family Court Advisory\nand Rules Committee.\n\n3.  \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill authorizes the Family Court to hear applications to change\nchildren's names, so that parties in Family Court are no longer forced\nto go to a second court to file a proceeding for a name change.\n\n4.  \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Family Court Act § 115 to provide that the\nFamily Court has jurisdiction to hear applications for changes of chil-\ndren's names, as set forth in Civil Rights Law § 60.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends Family Court Act § 439 to provide that\nSupport Magistrates may hear applications to change children's names as\nauthorized by Civil Rights Law § 60.\n\nSection 3 of the bill amends Civil Rights Law § 60 to authorize the\nFamily Court to hear name-change applications regarding children who are\nthe subject of, or parties to, pending proceedings in Family Court.\nApplications may be made by attorneys for the children or by any party.\n\nSection 4 of the bill amends Civil Rights Law § 62 to provide that the\nFamily Court may only grant name-change applications if all parties\nconsent.\n\nSection 5 of the bill sets the bill's effective date as the first day of\nApril following enactment.\n\n5.  \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nJurisdiction over petitions to change the names of children is vested by\nCivil Rights Law § 60 in just three courts in New York State:  Supreme\nCourt, County Court, and the New York City Civil Court.  However, there\nare cases in the Family Court where a change of name for a subject child\nwould be in the child's best interests, and it would further judicial\nefficiency if Family Court could authorize such a change of name as part\nof the proceeding involving the child - a proceeding in which the jurist\nmay have already heard evidence regarding the child's best interests and\nin which the parties and the child may already have legal represen-\ntation.\n\nCases of this sort often arise in paternity proceedings where, upon\ndetermining the parentage of the child, the jurist must instruct the\nparties to go to another court to file a new proceeding in order to\nobtain a name change for the child. This extra step may be costly for\nthe parents, as it will inevitably involve the purchase of an index\nnumber and, in some cases, the expenditure of fees for attorneys, and it\nwill inevitably cause a delay in fully resolving the issues to the\ndetriment of the child's best interests.\n\nThe benefits of this approach were made clear 40 years ago in Matter of\nMaria E. v. Anthony E., 125 Misc.2d 933 (Fam. Ct., Bronx Co., 1984)\n(Sheindlin, J.), where the court stated as follows:\n\n      \"It is this Court's opinion, however, that the authority to enter-\n      tain an application for a name change when brought in conjunction\n      with th a paternity proceeding should be vested in the Family\n      Court. The absence of such legislation seems to promote duplicity\n      of litigation, is judicially uneconomical and is without any\n      substantive rationale. The Family Court was established for the\n      care and protection of the young. (Committee Comments, Joint\n      Legislative Committee on Court Reorganization, Report II, the\n      Family Court Act, p. 2). This Court was meant to be the one judi-\n      cial forum in which almost all problems of the family could be\n      heard and hopefully settled. (Matter of Fusco v. Roth, 100 Misc.2d\n      288, 418 N.Y.S.2d 900; Brooks v.  Willie, supra). In fact, the\n      Family Court has the authority to determine every aspect of a\n      child's life, to wit: paternity, support, custody and visitation.\n      To then have to refer the litigants to another Court on the issue\n      of the name of a child is wasteful and makes no sense. . . .\"\n\nThis bill seeks to eliminate this undue burden on Family Court partic-\nipants, as well as the unnecessary burden on the courts, by authorizing\nthe Family Court to hear a limited class of name-change applications -\nthat is, where an application to change the name of a child under 18 is\nmade in a pending, related proceeding and where all parties consent to\nthe change. The attorney for the child or any of the parties may make\nthe application.\n\n6.  \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone. This is a new proposal.\n\n7.  \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill would have no meaningful State fiscal impact.\n\n8.  \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect on the first day of April following enact-\nment.\n",
